      "quote": "Efficacy and effectiveness of COVID-19 vaccines against SARS-CoV-2 infection: interim results of a living systematic review, 1 January to 14 May 2021",
      "reason": "The title explicitly includes the phrase \"living systematic review,\" identifying the report as a systematic review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "Efficacy and effectiveness of COVID-19 vaccines against SARS-CoV-2 infection: interim results of a living systematic review, 1 January to 14 May 2021",
      "reason": "The title states the population/subject (SARS-CoV-2 infection) and the intervention/exposure (COVID-19 vaccines), providing key information about the review question."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_1_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "Efficacy and effectiveness of COVID-19 vaccines against SARS-CoV-2 infection: interim results of a living systematic review, 1 January to 14 May 2021",
      "reason": "The title indicates supplementary information about update status and review type by stating this is an \"interim\" report of a \"living systematic review\" with dates, satisfying the conditional requirement."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_1",
      "quote": "Vaccination against severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) infection plays a key role in the containment of the coronavirus disease (COVID19) pandemic. All vaccines approved by the European Medical Agency (EMA) at the time of writing demonstrated high vaccine efficacy/effectiveness (VE) against severe COVID-19. With vaccination programmes being implemented in most European countries, it becomes urgent to assess the extent to which these vaccines are also able to prevent symptomatic and asymptomatic infections to guide public health recommendations and develop strategies for fully vaccinated people.",
      "reason": "Provides the current state (EMA-approved vaccines show high efficacy vs severe COVID-19) and identifies an important uncertainty/knowledge gap (extent to which vaccines prevent symptomatic and asymptomatic infections) motivating the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "Evidence on COVID-19 vaccine efficacy/effectiveness (VE) in preventing asymptomatic SARS-CoV-2 infections is needed to guide public health recommendations for vaccinated people. We report interim results of a living systematic review.",
      "reason": "Explicitly states the importance and need for the review to guide public health recommendations and reports interim results of a living systematic review addressing that need."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No evidence provided that discusses existing similar systematic reviews or explains the specific need for this review compared to prior reviews; no citations of prior reviews or statements about updating/replicating previous reviews were found in the supplied evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "VE against infection can also be regarded as an indicator of how well a vaccine prevents transmission. In addition, studies suggest that persons who become SARS-CoV-2-positive despite vaccination had a shorter duration of virus shedding and lower viral load [8].",
      "reason": "Provides partial mechanistic information (reduced viral load and shorter shedding as explanations for reduced transmission) but does not explicitly describe the vaccine's mechanism of action (e.g., immune response pathways); therefore only partial."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review concerns vaccine efficacy/effectiveness; there is no indication in the supplied evidence that the intervention is a complex, multi-component delivery context requiring a logic model, so this conditional item does not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "85"
}

{
